#!/usr/bin/env bun
// Writes or checks vendor/<crate>/UPSTREAM.patch, the whole diff between a vendored crate and its upstream.
//
//   scripts/vendor/regen-patch [--all | CRATE...] [--check] [--fresh] [--base REV] [--upstream DIR]
//
// The upstream is not in the repository. It is reconstructed by reverse-applying (`git apply -R`) the UPSTREAM.patch
// committed at a base revision to that revision's copy of the crate. The default base is the merge base of HEAD and
// MERGE_HEAD during a merge, else of HEAD and origin/main. During a merge whose other side is main's (contained in
// origin/main), main's upstream wins where it differs from the base's: main fixed a stale patch the base has, or has
// a stale one, which the merge then keeps as main has it. The working tree's UPSTREAM.patch is never read to make the
// upstream, so conflict markers in it do not matter: after merging main, resolve the source conflicts and run
// `scripts/vendor/regen-patch --all` (bench/wpt/README.md, "Merging main into a branch").
//
//   (default)  Writes UPSTREAM.patch for the working tree in the crate's committed style (CRATES below), then checks
//              it. A file whose contents equal its contents at HEAD, MERGE_HEAD or the base keeps that revision's
//              section of the patch byte for byte (when that revision's patch rebuilds the same upstream copy of the
//              file), so a merge changes only the sections of files both sides edited (and those of files whose
//              committed section was stale); every other section is regenerated.
//   --fresh    Regenerates every section. On main this reproduces the committed patches byte for byte (stylo's
//              timestamps, file times git does not keep, are carried over from the committed sections), except 9
//              `index` lines of taffy's, which name its files as they were before the rename to Pyro (#308).
//   --check    Writes nothing. Checks that the working tree's UPSTREAM.patch, applied with `git apply` to the
//              reconstructed upstream, reproduces the crate's files, and that it reverse-applies to them. Exit 1 if
//              not. CI runs `--check --all --base HEAD^1` on a pull request's merge commit
//              (.github/workflows/vendor-patches.yml). Without --base on main itself it proves little: HEAD is its
//              own base there, so the upstream is rebuilt from the very patch being checked.
//   --all      Every crate with an UPSTREAM.patch.
//   --base REV The revision whose committed patch and sources make the upstream (main's side of a merge is then not
//              preferred). A crate whose README.pyro.md title (its upstream) is not that revision's is rebuilt from
//              a revision that has the working tree's title, HEAD first; with none, the crate stops, unless --base
//              was given (a title edited for its wording).
//   --upstream DIR  Also writes each crate's reconstructed upstream (the covered files) to DIR/<crate>.
//
// The other revisions (HEAD, MERGE_HEAD) should reconstruct the same upstream; where one's committed patch does not,
// it is reported, and its sections of those files are not reused. A crate is refused while a covered file has
// conflict markers, is unmerged in the index (a modify/delete conflict leaves no markers) or is untracked (git add
// it, or delete it: a backup a merge tool left?).
//
// blitz-paint's, parley_engine's and stylo's sections are Apple `diff -u` output (macOS's /usr/bin/diff). Elsewhere a
// GNU diff can align some hunks differently: `git apply` still reproduces the sources, but the sections are noisier.
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';
